[h1]Getting Started[/h1]

This blog is a template. Every page is plain HTML, CSS and a little JavaScript, and posts are written in BBCode and turned into HTML by [url link=https://github.com/Bubu-Droid/bloops]bloops[/url]. Nothing runs on the server, so any host that serves static files will do. This post covers making the template yours, writing a post and publishing it.

[h2]What you need[/h2]

[list]
[*][url link=https://docs.astral.sh/uv/]uv[/url], to install bloops and prek, and to run the build script with a suitable Python.[/*]
[*][url link=https://git-scm.com/]Git[/url], and a host for static sites. The template comes set up for [url link=https://vercel.com/]Vercel[/url].[/*]
[*][url link=https://asymptote.sourceforge.io/]Asymptote[/url], only if your posts draw diagrams. Bloops calls [code mode=inline]asy[/code] to compile them to SVG.[/*]
[/list]

Install the two command-line tools once. Each gets its own isolated environment, and [code mode=inline]uv tool upgrade --all[/code] updates them later.

[code lang=bash]
uv tool install bloops
uv tool install prek

# or, without uv (needs Python 3.12+)
pip install bloops prek
[/code]

Either way, you then have the [code mode=inline]bloops[/code] and [code mode=inline]prek[/code] commands used below.

[h2]Make it yours[/h2]

[list type=ol]
[*]Create a repository from this template and clone it.[/*]
[*]Replace [code mode=inline]Your Blog Name[/code] in [code mode=inline]index.html[/code]. It appears twice: in the page title and in the heading.[/*]
[*]Replace the favicon with your own, described below.[/*]
[*]Delete the example posts: remove their folders and their entries in [code mode=inline]posts.json[/code]. With no posts, the home page says so.[/*]
[*]Install the git hooks, described below.[/*]
[/list]

Colours are Catppuccin tokens at the top of [code mode=inline]style.css[/code], for the home page, and of [code mode=inline]post.css[/code], for posts. Both files follow the reader's light or dark preference, and the button in the corner flips it.

The favicon is [code mode=inline]favicon.svg[/code], with [code mode=inline]favicon.ico[/code] as a fallback for browsers that cannot show SVG icons. To use your own, replace [code mode=inline]favicon.svg[/code] and make the fallback from it with [url link=https://imagemagick.org/]ImageMagick[/url]:

[code lang=bash]
magick -background none favicon.svg \
  -define icon:auto-resize=48,32,16 favicon.ico
[/code]

The posts need no changes: bloops gives every page its own default icon, and the build script swaps it for these two files.

[h2]How it is laid out[/h2]

[code lang=plaintext]
your-blog/
├── index.html          home page and post list
├── style.css           home page styles
├── post.css            styles for every post
├── post.js             theme toggle, sidenotes
├── favicon.svg         the blog's icon
├── favicon.ico         icon for older browsers
├── posts.json          slug, date, excerpt
├── build.py            lists posts on index.html
├── prek.toml           the git hooks
├── ruff.toml           build script lint rules
├── tombi.toml          TOML format rules
├── rumdl.toml          Markdown lint rules
├── vercel.json         URLs and security headers
└── my-post/
    ├── content.bbcode  the post, in BBCode
    ├── index.html      generated by bloops
    └── static/
        ├── style.css   imports ../../post.css
        ├── script.js   loads ../../post.js
        └── diagram.svg Asymptote diagrams
[/code]

Because each post's stylesheet and script are stubs pointing at the shared files, changing the look of every post means editing one file. The [code mode=inline]post.css[/code] and [code mode=inline]post.js[/code] here are copies of bloops' own [code mode=inline]static/style.css[/code] and [code mode=inline]static/script.js[/code]; copy them over again to pick up a newer bloops style.

[h2]Writing a post[/h2]

[list type=ol]
[*]Make a folder named after the post's slug, which becomes its address: lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, such as [code mode=inline]my-post[/code].[/*]
[*]Copy [code mode=inline]static/style.css[/code] and [code mode=inline]static/script.js[/code] from an existing post into [code mode=inline]my-post/static/[/code]. Bloops only writes its own copies of these when they are missing, so the stubs survive every rebuild.[/*]
[*]Write [code mode=inline]my-post/content.bbcode[/code], starting with the title as a level-one heading. The build script takes the post's title from there. The Bloops Showcase post shows every tag bloops knows; its [code mode=inline]content.bbcode[/code] is a good reference.[/*]
[*]Build the post with bloops.[/*]
[/list]

[code lang=bash]
bloops -pc -i my-post  # live preview
bloops -i my-post      # build once
[/code]

Always edit [code mode=inline]content.bbcode[/code], never the generated [code mode=inline]index.html[/code]: every build writes the page again from the source, and changes made by hand are lost.

[h2]Listing it on the home page[/h2]

Add an entry for the post to [code mode=inline]posts.json[/code]. All three fields are required. The date is written as year-month-day, and the list on the home page is sorted newest first. The excerpt is the preview shown under the title.

[code lang=json]
{
  "slug": "my-post",
  "date": "2026-10-28",
  "excerpt": "What the post is about."
}
[/code]

Then run the build script:

[code lang=bash]
uv run build.py   # uv picks the Python
python3 build.py  # needs Python 3.12+
[/code]

It writes the post list on the home page with each post's reading time, copies every post's title from its heading into [code mode=inline]posts.json[/code], and sets each post's browser-tab title and favicon, which bloops leaves as placeholders. Run it again after every bloops build. If an entry is wrong, for example a missing excerpt, a date that does not exist or a slug with no built page, it stops and says which entry and what is wrong.

When bloops itself is updated, its page template may change too, and pages built with the old version fall behind. Rebuild every post from its [code mode=inline]content.bbcode[/code] in one go:

[code lang=bash]
uv run build.py --rebuild
[/code]

This runs bloops on every post folder, then updates the home page as usual. It stops at the first post bloops cannot build and shows the error. Hand edits to generated pages are lost, which is one more reason to edit only the BBCode.

It runs the [code mode=inline]bloops[/code] command it finds on your PATH. To use a different one, such as a local checkout of bloops you are working on, set [code mode=inline]BLOOPS[/code] to the command that runs it:

[code lang=bash]
BLOOPS="uv run --project ../bloops bloops" \
  uv run build.py --rebuild
[/code]

[h2]Previewing the whole site[/h2]

[code mode=inline]bloops -pc[/code] previews one post at a time. To click through the home page and every post together, run a local server from the blog's root folder, the one holding the home page's [code mode=inline]index.html[/code]:

[code lang=bash]
python3 -m http.server
python3 -m http.server -d path/to/blog  # elsewhere
[/code]

Then open [url link=http://localhost:8000]localhost:8000[/url]. Posts are served at the same addresses as on the live site, such as [code mode=inline]/my-post/[/code].

[h2]The git hooks[/h2]

[url link=https://prek.j178.dev/]prek[/url] runs checks on every commit and push. Install the hooks once in each clone:

[code lang=bash]
prek install
[/code]

On every commit, it formats and checks the files you changed: [url link=https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/]ruff[/url] and [url link=https://docs.basedpyright.com/]basedpyright[/url] for the build script, [url link=https://github.com/rvben/rumdl]rumdl[/url] for Markdown, [url link=https://tombi-toml.github.io/tombi/]tombi[/url] for TOML, and [url link=https://prettier.io/]prettier[/url] for the home page, styles and scripts. [url link=https://github.com/codespell-project/codespell]codespell[/url] looks for common misspellings, and a set of [url link=https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit-hooks]general checks[/url] trims trailing whitespace, ends files with a newline, and catches merge-conflict markers and private keys. Post pages and their compiled diagrams are left alone, since bloops writes them again on every build. If a formatter changes a file, the commit stops; add the changes and commit again.

It also checks each commit message against [url link=https://www.conventionalcommits.org/]Conventional Commits[/url]: a type such as [code mode=inline]feat[/code], [code mode=inline]fix[/code] or [code mode=inline]docs[/code], an optional scope, then a summary, as in [code mode=inline]feat(posts): add a post on graphs[/code]. A message that does not follow it stops the commit.

Before every push, it reruns the build script in check mode. If the post list or a title was out of date, the script fixes it and the push stops, so you can commit the result and push again.

prek downloads every tool the hooks need the first time they run, Node for prettier included, so none of them has to be installed. The build check runs in a Python environment prek sets up itself, so it works even when the system Python is older than 3.12.

[h2]Publishing[/h2]

Import the repository on Vercel. There is no build step and no framework preset to pick: the files are served as they are, and every push to the main branch deploys. The sources, the build script and the hook configuration are listed in [code mode=inline].vercelignore[/code], so only the pages, styles, scripts and diagrams are uploaded. Any other static host works too; it only needs to serve [code mode=inline]my-post/index.html[/code] at [code mode=inline]/my-post/[/code].

[list type=ol]
[*]Build the post with bloops.[/*]
[*]Add it to [code mode=inline]posts.json[/code] and run the build script.[/*]
[*]Commit and push.[/*]
[/list]
